Self, played by Shepard Fairey.

10
00:39:05,454 --> 00:39:11,454
Self, played by INVADER.

11
00:43:00,000 --> 00:43:06,000
Self, played by Debora Guetta.

12
00:46:54,545 --> 00:47:00,545
Exit Through the Gift Shop is a 2010 British documentary
film directed by street artist Banksy.

13
00:50:49,090 --> 00:50:55,090
It tells the story of Thierry Guetta, a French immigrant in Los Angeles who, over several years, filmed a host of
street artists at work, including Shepard Fairey and Banksy, but failed to do anything with the footage.

14
00:54:43,636 --> 00:54:49,636
Eventually, Banksy decided to use the footage to make a documentary, which
includes new footage depicting Guetta's rise to fame as the artist "Mr.

15
00:58:38,181 --> 00:58:44,181
Brainwash".

16
01:02:32,727 --> 01:02:38,727
In addition to narration read by Rhys Ifans, the story is largely related by Banksy
himself, whose face is obscured and voice altered to preserve his anonymity.

17
01:06:27,272 --> 01:06:33,272
Geoff Barrow composed the film's score, and Richard Hawley's "Tonight The
Streets Are Ours" plays during the opening and closing credits.

18
01:10:21,818 --> 01:10:27,818
The film premiered at the 2010 Sundance Film Festival on 24 January 2010, and was
nominated for Best Documentary Feature at the 83rd Academy Awards.

19
01:14:16,363 --> 01:14:22,363
Since its release, there has been extensive debate over whether the
film is a genuine documentary or a mockumentary.

20
01:18:10,909 --> 01:18:16,909
When asked if the film was real, Banksy simply replied, "Yes."

21
01:22:05,454 --> 01:22:11,454
The film explores themes of painting, graffiti, fraud, cousin, street artist.
